{
  "case_id": "2017-17-43",
  "document_hash": "c1e50778fe2020797859a3344348f89f5516d580f24e1d7710bf06265ae3f6ab",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2017-17-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c1e50778fe2020797859a3344348f89f5516d580f24e1d7710bf06265ae3f6ab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c1e50778fe20-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:12 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Kannon K. Shanmugam",
      "case_id": "2017-17-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c1e50778fe2020797859a3344348f89f5516d580f24e1d7710bf06265ae3f6ab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c1e50778fe20-0000-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Title III directs the suppression of the evidence derived from a wiretap order when there is an error in obtaining, issuing, or executing that order."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2017-17-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c1e50778fe2020797859a3344348f89f5516d580f24e1d7710bf06265ae3f6ab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c1e50778fe20-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The government seems to make the argument now that these orders were not insufficient on their face because they were merely overbroad."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Kannon K. Shanmugam",
      "case_id": "2017-17-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c1e50778fe2020797859a3344348f89f5516d580f24e1d7710bf06265ae3f6ab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c1e50778fe20-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We believe that the orders would still be insufficient if they did not contain that; in other words, we believe that the better reading of this statute is that it requires the jurisdictional limitation to appear on the face of the order."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2017-17-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c1e50778fe2020797859a3344348f89f5516d580f24e1d7710bf06265ae3f6ab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c1e50778fe20-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SHANMUGAM: Well, in fact, there were. There were communications that were intercepted from a wire room in St. Louis. In response to the motion to suppress, the government, I think quite wisely, indicated that it was not going to introduce those communications. But I think, more broadly, I would say two things about that, Justice Ginsburg. First, I would say that when you have a facially insufficient order, any communications that are intercepted pursuant to that order are unlawfully intercepted. I think that that was"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2017-17-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c1e50778fe2020797859a3344348f89f5516d580f24e1d7710bf06265ae3f6ab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c1e50778fe20-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: But there was no invalid application. The only intercepts that were introduced were in -- from the District of Kansas. So the -- the order authorized an invalid application, but there was no such invalid application."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2017-17-43",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c1e50778fe2020797859a3344348f89f5516d580f24e1d7710bf06265ae3f6ab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c1e50778fe20-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: I'm a little bit still worried about what we discovered at the outset, that at least they are claiming that this order has to say you have authority to wiretap in Kansas as well as outside."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2017-17-43",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "c1e50778fe2020797859a3344348f89f5516d580f24e1d7710bf06265ae3f6ab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c1e50778fe20-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Mr. Cox, just so I understand your argument, your argument really is that the core -- the court's approach was right, that the core concern analysis applies to (ii) and (iii), that what we announced in Giordano and -- and -- was it Chavez?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2017-17-43",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c1e50778fe2020797859a3344348f89f5516d580f24e1d7710bf06265ae3f6ab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c1e50778fe20-0002-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: No, I don't want to know those terms. I want what the circuit split is."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2017-17-43",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c1e50778fe2020797859a3344348f89f5516d580f24e1d7710bf06265ae3f6ab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c1e50778fe20-0002-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Mr. Tripp -- I'm sorry, finish your sentence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government (Mr. Tripp)",
      "case_id": "2017-17-43",
      "confidence": 0.93,
      "document_hash": "c1e50778fe2020797859a3344348f89f5516d580f24e1d7710bf06265ae3f6ab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c1e50778fe20-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. TRIPP: No, I don't think that's the upshot of our position. I think one thing to just point out, as a practical matter, these orders contain a large amount of material that Title III does not actually require them to contain in order for interception of the communications to lawfully occur under Title III."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2017-17-43",
      "confidence": 0.96,
      "document_hash": "c1e50778fe2020797859a3344348f89f5516d580f24e1d7710bf06265ae3f6ab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c1e50778fe20-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Mr. Tripp, one thing that -- one thing that the statute requires is that the order identify the person authorizing the wiretap. Now let's say you put your name down, Zachary Tripp, Assistant to the Solicitor General. You have satisfied the statute. You have identified the person authorizing the wiretap, but you're not allowed to do that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2017-17-43",
      "confidence": 0.93,
      "document_hash": "c1e50778fe2020797859a3344348f89f5516d580f24e1d7710bf06265ae3f6ab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c1e50778fe20-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: On that one point, might it have been true that you did use some of the California intercepts from -- that originate from Missouri as part of your general investigation?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Mr. Shanmugam)",
      "case_id": "2017-17-43",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c1e50778fe2020797859a3344348f89f5516d580f24e1d7710bf06265ae3f6ab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c1e50778fe20-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SHANMUGAM: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice. The district court in this case exceeded its power under Title III by authorizing what was effectively a nationwide wiretap for the phones at issue."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2017-17-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c1e50778fe2020797859a3344348f89f5516d580f24e1d7710bf06265ae3f6ab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c1e50778fe20-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: I mean, our legal system usually does not give a party relief based on a mistake that had absolutely no effect on the party. Now I understand that the provision that's directly at issue here is an exception to that. It is a -- it is a list. It's some kind of a -- there's some requirements that have to be in the warrant, and whether or not they had any effect whatsoever, the statute says that there has to be suppression. But when you have that kind of a rule Heritage Reporting Corporation"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2017-17-43",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c1e50778fe2020797859a3344348f89f5516d580f24e1d7710bf06265ae3f6ab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c1e50778fe20-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 69,
        "start": 69
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, counsel. The case is submitted."
    },
    {
